  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest satellite and radar imagery reveal an area of \nwidespread cloud cover and rain slowly moving east through the \nMidwest and Lower Great Lakes this afternoon along an arcing \nbaroclinic zone, well ahead of a stronger upper-level trough and\nsurface cold front. Generally light rain is expected with this \nactivity later this evening and overnight as it sags east, \nmainly along the lakeshore.\n\nThe more steady and widespread rain will arrive Thursday night into \nFriday as the surface cold front interacts with a potent mid-level \nshortwave. This will bring a narrow area of deep moisture profiles \ninto the area, with PWATs reaching 1.8 to 2.0 inches at times. Given \nthe recent dry pattern across the region (3-hr FFGs averaging 2 to \n2.5 inches), not anticipating any significant water issues, though \ncan't rule out some isolated nuisance flooding, especially along or \nwest of the I-71 corridor where low probabilities exist (10 to 30%) \nfor 6-hr rain amounts of 1 to 2 inches. Elsewhere, rainfall amounts \nshould generally remain at or below 1 inch, but nonetheless, a good \nsoaking rain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nFollowing a brief spell of high pressure behind the first cold \nfront Friday into Saturday, another reinforcing cold front will \nmove south through the area on Sunday as a stronger area of \nCanadian high pressure builds south across the Upper Midwest and\nGreat Lakes. Below average temperatures and dry weather is \nfavored with this pattern with highs generally in the upper 50s \nto 60s and lows in the upper 30s to 40s. This pattern could \nbring our first widespread Frost/Freeze threat, especially \nMonday and Tuesday nights where light winds and clear skies will\nresult in efficient radiative cooling.",
